<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Impact of Typography Description.</span>
We further evaluate <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VII w/o typographic description</span>, which retains the visual symbols while removing the typographic description, and instead directly uses the typographic description (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{desc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{desc}</annotation></semantics></math>) as the text prompt to define the semantics.
The results show that the typographic description generated by MIR is indispensable for defining the semantics of the malicious action. Removing the printed text consistently lowers the ASR (e.g., dropping from 83.5% to 56.0% on PixVerse-V5).
This confirms that while visual symbols provide necessary spatial attention, the <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">typographic injection</span> is required to forcefully override the semantic interpretation of the scene.
Ultimately, the superior performance of VII stems from the synergistic combination of <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">spatial grounding</span> via VIG and <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semantic definition</span> via MIR.</p>
</div>
